WEBVTT

00:00.090 --> 00:01.610
Well done for getting this far.

00:01.750 --> 00:03.420
It's hard work,

00:03.690 --> 00:04.820
but keep going.

00:05.240 --> 00:06.119
You can do it,

00:06.119 --> 00:08.403
and you will have learnt a lot more by

00:08.403 --> 00:09.750
the end of this course.

00:10.050 --> 00:13.355
You only need two sentence patterns to

00:13.355 --> 00:16.407
describe almost any change for line

00:16.407 --> 00:17.000
graphs.

00:17.400 --> 00:19.846
There are two main sentence structures

00:19.846 --> 00:20.974
that you can use.

00:20.974 --> 00:23.608
Let me show you both with one increase on

00:23.608 --> 00:24.110
a graph.

00:24.410 --> 00:26.745
This graph shows how many passengers used

00:26.745 --> 00:28.302
a ferry service each month,

00:28.302 --> 00:29.470
from January to June.

00:30.150 --> 00:31.480
Look at March to April.

00:31.900 --> 00:34.626
Passenger numbers jumped from 51,000 to

00:34.626 --> 00:36.330
70,000 in just one month.

00:37.260 --> 00:39.455
Pattern one is the verb pattern.

00:39.455 --> 00:41.450
Let's build it piece by piece.

00:41.770 --> 00:43.869
What changed? Passenger numbers.

00:43.869 --> 00:44.950
What did they do?

00:45.430 --> 00:46.160
They rose.

00:46.560 --> 00:46.970
How?

00:47.630 --> 00:50.657
Sharply, because the line is steep.

00:50.657 --> 00:54.020
Then the figures: from 51,000 to 70,000,

00:54.830 --> 00:57.570
and the time: between March and April.

00:57.960 --> 01:00.146
So then you'll get the sentence:

01:00.146 --> 01:02.200
“Passenger numbers rose sharply

01:02.470 --> 01:06.623
from 51,000 to 70,000 between March and

01:06.623 --> 01:07.350
April.”

01:08.510 --> 01:09.960
So you say what changed,

01:10.320 --> 01:10.780
how,

01:11.180 --> 01:13.228
the figures, and then the time.

01:13.228 --> 01:15.276
And you will use the verb rose,

01:15.276 --> 01:16.300
or increased, or

01:16.910 --> 01:19.830
soared, or rocketed, or plummeted,

01:19.830 --> 01:22.334
or plunged. Most of the time,

01:22.334 --> 01:24.670
line graphs are in the past.

01:25.120 --> 01:27.571
So I often give examples in the past,

01:27.571 --> 01:30.215
but sometimes they are in the present or

01:30.215 --> 01:32.214
the future, normally. So yeah,

01:32.214 --> 01:34.020
usually in the past, though.

01:34.270 --> 01:36.722
Pattern two is the noun pattern.

01:36.722 --> 01:38.802
It starts with “There was”.

01:38.802 --> 01:41.254
The adverb “sharply” becomes the

01:41.254 --> 01:42.666
adjective “sharp”,

01:42.666 --> 01:45.415
and the verb “rose” becomes the noun

01:45.415 --> 01:46.306
“rise”. So:

01:46.306 --> 01:49.055
“There was a sharp rise in passenger

01:49.055 --> 01:51.953
numbers…” I'll let you finish that one

01:51.953 --> 01:52.250
off.

01:52.530 --> 01:54.084
Never mix the two.

01:54.084 --> 01:57.274
It's either “rose sharply” or “a sharp

01:57.274 --> 01:59.400
rise”. Never “rose sharp”.

01:59.740 --> 02:01.412
As an examiner,

02:01.412 --> 02:05.904
I saw one small slip quite often: “rised”.

02:05.904 --> 02:09.770
Rise is irregular. Rise, rose, risen.

02:10.460 --> 02:12.279
Write “rose”, like the flower,

02:12.279 --> 02:13.100
never “rised”.

02:13.460 --> 02:16.019
Using both patterns gives you range,

02:16.019 --> 02:18.440
and range lifts your grammar score.

02:18.740 --> 02:19.819
On the right page,

02:19.819 --> 02:22.148
build the verb sentence from the pieces.

02:22.148 --> 02:23.568
Three of them are wrong,

02:23.568 --> 02:24.590
so look carefully.

02:25.460 --> 02:28.085
Then rewrite it with the noun pattern,

02:28.085 --> 02:30.440
keeping the figures and the months.
